<main class="sheet">
    <%- partial("layouts/top", {"here": "gaps"}) %>

    <section class="hero">
        <div class="mark">Gaps<span>&sect;04.9</span></div>
        <div>
            <h1>What is not there yet.</h1>
            <p class="lede">
                Writing a renderer means rewriting what a browser gives away: text
                shaping, input methods, accessibility, selection. That is the real cost of
                this project and it is not hidden in a later milestone. This page is the
                other half of the catalogue &mdash; the things a desktop application
                expects, each one with where it would be fixed and whether anything is
                actually blocking it. Five of them are closed now, and the page keeps
                those rather than deleting them: what a gap cost to shut is the most
                useful thing it leaves behind.
            </p>

            <nav class="toc">
                <a href="#ledger"><b>The ledger</b><span>twenty-two needs, scored</span></a>
                <a href="#icons"><b>Icons</b><span>the first one closed</span></a>
                <a href="#events"><b>Six dead events</b><span>on the wire, nowhere else</span></a>
                <a href="#menus"><b>Context menus</b><span>the event without the anchor</span></a>
                <a href="#keyboard"><b>Accelerators</b><span>still no global key capture</span></a>
                <a href="#layout"><b>Layout, stated</b><span>what version 1 leaves out</span></a>
                <a href="#splits"><b>Split panes</b><span>the second one closed</span></a>
                <a href="#a11y"><b>Accessibility</b><span>the third one closed</span></a>
                <a href="#keys"><b>Keyboard</b><span>escape, traps and claims</span></a>
                <a href="#blocked"><b>Blocked or unwritten</b><span>the distinction that matters</span></a>
            </nav>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="ledger">
        <div class="mark">Ledger<span>today</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2>Twenty-two things a desktop application expects</h2>
            <p>
                Scored against what the client does now, not against what the catalogue
                names. A widget that exists but cannot be reached from the keyboard is
                partial, not present.
            </p>

            <table class="ledger gaps">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>need</th><th>where it stands</th><th>state</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>icons</td><td>Nineteen icons under twenty-two names, stroked from the client's own table; an unknown name draws nothing and keeps its space</td><td class="yes">built</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>drag &amp; drop</td><td>Three wire events, none emitted; no OS file drop</td><td class="no">absent</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>context menu</td><td>The event fires on right-click; no widget, and no way to anchor at the pointer</td><td class="part">partial</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>command palette</td><td>Needs an accelerator to open it, and there is no global key capture</td><td class="no">absent</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>focus trap</td><td>A <code>modal</code> prop makes its subtree the whole Tab order, and nests</td><td class="yes">built</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>escape to close</td><td>Reaches a handler on the path that asked for it; blurs only when none did</td><td class="yes">built</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>accelerators</td><td>Keys reach only a focused node holding <code>key_down</code></td><td class="no">absent</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>arrow navigation</td><td>A widget can now take the arrows and keep Enter, but there is still no roving focus and no type-ahead</td><td class="part">partial</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>a11y roles</td><td>Declared in props and preferred over the kind; 35 role names, states, values and set positions</td><td class="yes">built</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>overlay dismissal</td><td>No light-dismiss, no close on scroll or focus-out</td><td class="no">absent</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>overlay placement</td><td>One shape: below, left-aligned, flipped up, clamped</td><td class="part">partial</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>sticky headers</td><td>Sticky positioning is out of layout version 1</td><td class="no">absent</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>grid tracks</td><td><code>N</code> equal columns only; no spanning, no named areas</td><td class="part">partial</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>text selection</td><td>Inside an editable node only &mdash; a label cannot be selected</td><td class="part">partial</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>horizontal scrollbar</td><td>The thumb is computed for the vertical axis alone</td><td class="no">absent</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>exit animation</td><td>The accelerate curve is defined and used by nothing</td><td class="no">absent</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>density / font scale</td><td>Plumbed through theme resolution and never set</td><td class="no">inert</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>RTL mirroring</td><td>Shaping is bidirectional; layout mirroring is out of version 1</td><td class="no">absent</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>split panes</td><td>Draggable dividers on both axes, keyboard-movable, panels sized in px</td><td class="yes">built</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>virtualised lists</td><td>Windowed, with heights the tree does not hold</td><td class="yes">built</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>light / dark / palette</td><td>Resolved from roles, contrast by construction, follows the desktop</td><td class="yes">built</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>input methods</td><td>Preedit anchored to the field, commit as one <code>text_input</code></td><td class="yes">built</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="icons">
        <div class="mark">Icons<span>built</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2>The kind that used to paint nothing</h2>
            <p>
                <code>icon</code> had been one of the sixteen primitives since the first
                protocol version, sized by layout like a picture and painted by nothing:
                the painter&rsquo;s match on node kind had no arm for it, so it fell
                through and left a hole of the right size. The catalogue worked around it
                with characters &mdash; a chevron was <code>&#9662;</code>, a tick was
                <code>&#10003;</code> &mdash; which land on a 227&nbsp;KB fallback symbols
                face and are a poor icon three ways over: they cannot be sized against the
                control they sit in, cannot take a colour apart from their label, and
                reach a screen reader as themselves.
            </p>
            <p>
                There is an arm now, and a table of nineteen icons behind it, under
                twenty-two names &mdash; <code>dash</code>, <code>sort_asc</code> and
                <code>sort_desc</code> reach for a shape another name already draws.
                Every icon is
                polylines on a 24-unit grid with a 2-unit stroke, and every segment is the
                <strong>same rounded capsule a chart&rsquo;s line is made of</strong> — so
                it cost no new pipeline, no font, no asset fetch and no protocol version. A
                run of one point is a dot, because a capsule of no length is a circle of
                the stroke&rsquo;s own radius.
            </p>

            <div class="wire live">
                <div><b>chevron_down</b></div>
                <div><b>chevron_up</b></div>
                <div><b>chevron_left</b></div>
                <div><b>chevron_right</b></div>
                <div><b>check</b></div>
                <div><b>minus</b></div>
                <div><b>plus</b></div>
                <div><b>close</b></div>
                <div><b>menu</b></div>
                <div><b>arrow_up</b><i>sort_asc</i></div>
                <div><b>arrow_down</b><i>sort_desc</i></div>
                <div><b>arrow_left</b></div>
                <div><b>arrow_right</b></div>
                <div><b>more_h</b></div>
                <div><b>more_v</b></div>
                <div><b>dot</b></div>
                <div><b>search</b></div>
                <div><b>circle</b></div>
                <div><b>warning</b></div>
            </div>

            <p>
                An icon with no size of its own takes a square from the font size in
                force, so one beside a label needs no measurement from the server. An
                unknown name draws nothing and keeps its space &mdash; which is the rule
                that lets the set grow without a client release, and is a MUST in
                <a href="https://github.com/solisoft/eui/blob/main/spec/03-widgets.md">03 &sect;2</a>
                now rather than an accident of the implementation.
            </p>
            <p class="note">
                Nothing in the catalogue draws an icon as a character any more. The
                select&rsquo;s chevron, the accordion&rsquo;s and the tree&rsquo;s
                disclosures, the calendar&rsquo;s month arrows, the chip&rsquo;s remove
                and the checkbox&rsquo;s tick and dash are icons. A few call sites still
                <em>pass</em> a character &mdash; <code>icon_button</code> keeps taking
                one, so an application that has not moved yet still works &mdash; but
                where an <code>icon</code> is given beside it, the character is never
                drawn. Pagination gained something else on the way: its ends are disabled
                now, so the page cannot be walked past either end, which the character
                version had never stopped.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="events">
        <div class="mark">Events<span>&sect;06.1</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2>Six of the twenty-five are on the wire and nowhere else</h2>
            <p>
                The protocol names twenty-five event kinds. Nineteen are emitted by the
                client. The other six are decodable, documented, and never sent &mdash;
                a server may subscribe to them and will wait forever.
            </p>

            <div class="wire dead">
                <div><b>double_click</b><i>0x02</i></div>
                <div><b>resize</b><i>0x10</i></div>
                <div><b>drag_start</b><i>0x12</i></div>
                <div><b>drag_over</b><i>0x13</i></div>
                <div><b>drop</b><i>0x14</i></div>
                <div><b>long_press</b><i>0x15</i></div>
            </div>

            <p>
                Three of them are drag and drop, which is therefore absent end to end:
                no drag threshold, no payload, no drop-target highlighting, and no OS
                file drop into the window. The only real drags in the client today are
                its own and are never reported &mdash; the scrollbar thumb, and extending
                a selection inside a field.
            </p>
            <p class="note">
                It is worth being exact about which of these is a hole and which is a
                decision. <code>double_click</code> and <code>long_press</code> are
                conveniences a server can approximate from what it does receive.
                <code>drop</code> cannot be approximated by anything.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="menus">
        <div class="mark">Menus<span>&sect;04.5</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2>The event arrives; the anchor does not</h2>
            <p>
                Right-click <em>is</em> reported: a release of button&nbsp;1 becomes
                <code>context_menu</code> rather than <code>click</code>. What is missing
                is everywhere it would have to go.
            </p>

            <div class="split">
                <div>
                    <b>what anchoring can say</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li>An <code>overlay</code> inside a <code>stack</code> hangs off that stack&rsquo;s first in-flow child</li>
                        <li>Its left edge starts on the anchor&rsquo;s left edge</li>
                        <li>Its top is the anchor&rsquo;s bottom, plus its own top margin</li>
                        <li>It flips above when it would fall out of the viewport and there is more room up there</li>
                        <li>Then it is clamped into the viewport on both axes</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <b>what it cannot say</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Any other placement &mdash; no start, end, left, right, or centred</li>
                        <li>An offset, or an arrow that points back at the anchor</li>
                        <li>A different anchor: it is always the first in-flow sibling</li>
                        <li><strong>A position at the pointer</strong>, which is the one a context menu needs</li>
                        <li>Close on an outside click, on a scroll, or on focus leaving</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="note">
                And there is no <code>top</code>, <code>left</code>, <code>right</code> or
                <code>bottom</code> in the style record at all: absolute position is
                alignment plus margins. So a menu at the cursor cannot be expressed by
                going around the anchoring rule either.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="keyboard">
        <div class="mark">Keyboard<span>&sect;06.3</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2>There is no global key capture</h2>
            <p>
                That is the specification&rsquo;s own sentence, under the heading
                <em>What the client will not report</em>. Keystrokes reach a focused
                editable node, or a focused node that explicitly holds a
                <code>key_down</code> handler. Nothing else.
            </p>
            <p>
                Read it together with the driver and the consequence is sharp: if nothing
                has focus, no key is reported at all. <code>Ctrl+K</code>,
                <code>Ctrl+S</code>, <code>/</code> and <code>?</code> &mdash; the whole
                accelerator vocabulary of a desktop application &mdash; are unreachable,
                and a command palette cannot be opened by the gesture that opens command
                palettes.
            </p>
            <p>
                Escape has the matching problem from the other side. The client handles it
                before anything else and returns immediately: it drops focus, and the
                server never learns the key was pressed. So no dialog, sheet, drawer,
                popover or menu in the catalogue can close the way every one of them
                should.
            </p>
            <p class="note">
                Neither of these needs a protocol change. Escape reaching a
                <code>key_down</code> handler on the path, a <code>modal</code> prop that
                narrows the focus order to one subtree, and an <code>autofocus</code> prop
                to put focus inside it when it opens &mdash; three small changes in the
                client, and every overlay in the catalogue becomes usable from the
                keyboard.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="layout">
        <div class="mark">Layout<span>&sect;04.9</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2>Stated so no one goes looking</h2>
            <p>
                The layout specification has a section listing what it deliberately does
                not do in version 1. It is short, and every line of it is felt by some
                desktop pattern.
            </p>

            <div class="units">
                <div>
                    <b>no sticky positioning</b>
                    <p>
                        No sticky table header, no sticky section header in a list, no
                        toolbar pinned inside a scroller. The data grid keeps its header
                        <em>outside</em> the virtualised list instead, which works and is
                        the pattern to copy.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <b>grid: N equal columns</b>
                    <p>
                        No track sizing, no spanning, no named areas. A table with a
                        32&nbsp;px checkbox column, a flexible name and a 120&nbsp;px date
                        is built from rows with per-cell widths &mdash; which is why
                        resizing a column means re-emitting every row.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <b>no aspect-ratio</b>
                    <p>
                        Thumbnails, media placeholders and avatars in a responsive grid
                        need a height the server has worked out for them.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <b>no RTL mirroring</b>
                    <p>
                        Text shaping is bidirectional; layout is not. A leading edge is
                        always the left one.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <b>no cross-container stacking</b>
                    <p>
                        <code>z</code> orders siblings within one <code>stack</code>.
                        Between two overlays, order in the tree is the order on screen.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <b>no auto margins, no order</b>
                    <p>
                        Pushing one button to the right of a toolbar is a
                        <code>spacer</code> with grow, which the catalogue does
                        throughout.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="splits">
        <div class="mark">Splits<span>built</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2>Draggable dividers, on both axes</h2>
            <p>
                This page listed split panes as unwritten rather than blocked, and the
                distinction was worth something: they are written now, and nothing in the
                client changed to allow it.
            </p>
            <p>
                A press on the divider captures the pointer, so a drag that runs off it
                still arrives. The move and release handlers sit on the <em>container</em>
                rather than the divider, and the payload of a pointer event is measured
                against the node whose handler catches it &mdash; so the number reaching
                the server is already the divider&rsquo;s position inside its container,
                needing no arithmetic and no memory of where the drag began. Moves are
                coalesced to one per frame, not one per sample the mouse sends.
            </p>
            <p>
                The divider also holds <code>key_down</code>, which is what puts it in the
                Tab order: a split can be moved with the arrow keys, and recentred with
                Home, by someone who never touches a pointer.
            </p>

            <div class="split">
                <div>
                    <b>what it does</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Vertical and horizontal dividers, and they nest</li>
                        <li>A minimum size for each panel, clamped in one place</li>
                        <li>The fraction is per mille, so it survives state as an integer</li>
                        <li>Both conversions round, so a divider dropped on a pixel rebuilds on that pixel &mdash; checked across every pixel of travel</li>
                        <li>A separator role, an orientation, and the position as a value</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <b>content that answers its panel</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Each panel is built by a function of its own width in pixels</li>
                        <li>So a view can branch on the panel instead of the window</li>
                        <li><code>bp()</code> is no use here: its rungs are Tailwind&rsquo;s and a 309&nbsp;px panel and a 505&nbsp;px one are both <code>xs</code></li>
                        <li><code>pane_bp(px)</code> is the same idea at the scale a panel lives at &mdash; 200, 320, 480, 720</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="note">
                What is still missing is the latency: every frame of a drag is a round
                trip, because a local chunk cannot read the event that triggered it. On a
                loopback or a LAN that is invisible; over a long link it would not be.
                That is the chunk-payload gap below, and it is the one thing here that a
                protocol change would fix.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="a11y">
        <div class="mark">A11y<span>&sect;03.6</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2>A widget says what it is, instead of being guessed at</h2>
            <p>
                The mapping used to be kind and handler alone: anything with a
                <code>click</code> handler was a button named by the text inside it. So a
                checkbox, a switch, a tab, a menu item and a slider all reached AT-SPI,
                UIA and AX as &ldquo;Button&rdquo;, and none of them carried a state. A
                screen reader user could not tell a switch from a link.
            </p>
            <p>
                A node may now <strong>declare</strong> itself, in props the client reads:
                a role from thirty-five names, a label that overrides the text inside, and
                the states &mdash; checked (with its third value), expanded, selected,
                disabled, read-only, required, invalid, busy, modal &mdash; plus a value
                with its range, a place in a set, a level, an orientation and a live
                urgency. Props already travel, so this cost <strong>nothing on the
                wire</strong>: it is a change to one file in the client and a section of
                the specification.
            </p>

            <div class="split">
                <div>
                    <b>three rules, now normative</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Leafness follows the role, not the handler.</strong> A button or a tab is named by its text and exposed without children; a <code>tab_list</code>, a <code>menu</code> or a <code>grid</code> keeps what it holds — which the old rule swallowed</li>
                        <li><strong>A disabled node keeps its role.</strong> Disabling drops the handlers, so without a declaration there is no button left to infer and the control decays into an unnamed group</li>
                        <li><strong>A present zero is not an absence.</strong> <code>value_now: 0</code> is a slider at the bottom of its range</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <b>what the todo now says</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>CheckBox "Write the spec" checked=Yes</code></li>
                        <li><code>Button "Remove Write the spec"</code> — where it used to say <code>Button "×"</code></li>
                        <li><code>Tab "Overview" selected=true 1 of 3</code></li>
                        <li><code>Slider "Value" value=40</code></li>
                        <li><code>Button "Previous page" disabled</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="note">
                An unknown role name falls back to the kind rather than failing, which is
                what lets the vocabulary grow without a client release. Thirteen vectors
                hold the rules, including every role discriminant surviving the
                <code>to_u8</code>/<code>from_u8</code> round trip its journey across the
                worker&rsquo;s process boundary makes of it. The kind-mapping default is
                pinned by a separate test, so a tree that declares nothing is provably
                exposed exactly as it was before any of this existed.
            </p>
            <p class="note">
                <strong>Half-done, and worth saying so.</strong> The client reads all of
                it; the catalogue is still declaring its way through. Checkbox, switch,
                tabs, segmented, icon buttons, pagination, the calendar arrows, the
                chip&rsquo;s remove, slider, progress, toast and the split divider say
                what they are &mdash; a run of the gallery answers with six
                <code>TabList</code>s, sixteen <code>Tab</code>s, four
                <code>Separator</code>s, two <code>Slider</code>s and two
                <code>Progress</code>es where every one of those used to be a button or a
                group. Against that, three hundred and thirty-eight nodes are still plain
                buttons: menus, the select, the tree, the data grid, dialogs and the day
                cells have not been declared yet.
                And a real screen reader remains the only honest test &mdash;
                <code>SNAPSHOT_A11Y=1</code> on the off-screen renderer is the one that
                can run without one.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="keys">
        <div class="mark">Keyboard<span>&sect;03.1</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2>Four things a server cannot do for itself</h2>
            <p>
                It does not own <code>Tab</code>. It was never told about
                <code>Escape</code>. And it cannot know which node the client will treat
                as pressed. So a dialog could not trap focus, could not open with focus
                inside it, and could not close on the key every dialog closes on. Three
                props and one changed rule fix all four.
            </p>

            <div class="split">
                <div>
                    <b>what the client now reads</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>modal</code> &mdash; while it is laid out, the Tab order is <strong>its subtree alone</strong>. Innermost wins, so a dialog over a dialog traps in the second</li>
                        <li><code>autofocus</code> &mdash; focus starts here when the surface arrives, and is <em>not</em> reclaimed by a later batch: someone tabbing through an open dialog is not pulled back to its first field</li>
                        <li><code>keys</code> &mdash; the keys this node wants, and it is sent no others</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <b>and what that buys</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li>A tab can take <code>ArrowLeft</code> and <code>ArrowRight</code> and still be activated by <code>Enter</code> &mdash; which a <code>key_down</code> handler used to make impossible, because it claimed every key</li>
                        <li>A dialog can listen for <code>Escape</code> <strong>without hearing every letter typed into the field inside it</strong></li>
                        <li>Fewer round trips: a key a node did not ask for is not sent at all</li>
                        <li>A node with a handler and no <code>keys</code> prop still hears everything, so nothing that worked stops</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="note">
                <code>Escape</code> follows from the third. It reaches a handler on the
                path that asked for it, and focus is left where it was so the surface can
                put it back; with nothing listening it drops focus, which is what it has
                always done. Driven through a real server: the gallery&rsquo;s sheet opens
                at 940 nodes carrying <code>Dialog "A sheet" modal</code>, and one
                <code>Escape</code> later it is 934 and the dialog is gone.
            </p>
            <p class="note">
                Ten vectors hold it, and the off-screen renderer grew
                <code>SNAPSHOT_KEYS</code> so a focus ring, a trapped Tab or a surface
                that closes on a key can be looked at without a keyboard. What is still
                missing is above this: roving focus and type-ahead are a widget&rsquo;s
                to implement over the wire for now, one round trip per arrow, and
                accelerators need the global capture the specification still refuses.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="blocked">
        <div class="mark">Blocked<span>or not</span></div>
        <div>
            <h2>The distinction that actually matters</h2>
            <p>
                Most of this page is work, not impossibility. It is worth separating the
                two, because the status page had one of them filed wrong: column resize
                was recorded as blocked, and it is not.
            </p>

            <div class="split">
                <div>
                    <b>genuinely blocked</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Sticky headers</strong> &mdash; layout version&nbsp;1 has no sticky, and faking it needs a scroll offset the server does not have</li>
                        <li><strong>A masked password field</strong> &mdash; nothing in the client hides an <code>input</code>&rsquo;s glyphs</li>
                        <li><strong>Local keyboard navigation and local drag</strong> &mdash; a bytecode chunk cannot see the event that triggered it, so it never learns which key, or where the pointer is</li>
                        <li><strong>Control heights that follow density</strong> &mdash; the theme resolves them and no dimension on the wire can reference them</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <b>merely unwritten</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Column resize</strong> &mdash; the same server-driven drag <code>split_pane</code> now uses; what is left is re-emitting the row styles a width change touches</li>
                        <li><strong>A context menu widget</strong>, once anchoring can name a point</li>
                        <li><strong>textarea</strong> &mdash; the primitive is in the client and no builder wraps it</li>
                        <li><strong>link, radio_group, combobox, rating, time_picker, list_item, panel</strong> &mdash; ordinary compositions, waiting their turn</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="note">
                The rule the two columns come from: a thing is blocked when expressing it
                would need a new node kind, a new style key, a new event, or a chunk that
                can read its own event. Everything else is a function someone has not
                written yet.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</main>
